  "data_url": "https://huggingface.co/datasets/CHGGM-Aachen/genolator-v1-qa",
  "verified": {
    "opened_data_files": true,
    "what_i_actually_checked": "Downloaded all three 42 MB 'sample' parquet files (2,000 rows each) and read the full published train (345,927 rows) and test (35,387 rows) splits column-wise over HTTP range requests. Confirmed the 12 columns, the three embedding sizes per row (4,096 / 2,560 / 1,280 numbers), zero missing values, zero gene overlap between splits, and the yes/no label balance. Then ran the decisive test: a plain bag-of-words logistic regression trained on the question wording ALONE (no DNA, no protein sequence, no structure, no embeddings, no gene name) over the authors' own 230,226 train / 30,406 test yes-no questions. It scored accuracy 0.9724, F1 0.9729, MCC (Matthews correlation coefficient, a balanced accuracy measure from -1 to 1) 0.9449 - above every baseline the paper reports and level with the paper's second-best full model.",
    "what_came_from_paper_text_only": "Table 1 numbers quoted from the results text, not from the table itself: Genolator full Evo2+PST MCC 0.959, Evo2+ESM2 0.948, projector-only 0.924, XGBoost on structure plus BioBERT question embeddings 0.913, GPT-4.1 0.179. Also taken from the text only: how the questions were generated (GPT-4.1 with few-shot prompts), the k-means (a clustering method) similarity-aware splitting procedure, and the manual quality review of 56 genes. I did not run the released Genolator model itself (a fine-tuned Llama-3 8B, which needs a GPU)."
  },
  "part_a_killers": {
    "a1_downloadable": {"pass": true, "note": "Open on Hugging Face, no login or application. I downloaded 127 MB of sample files and streamed columns from the 7.2 GB train file. Code is MIT-licensed on GitHub; the dataset repo itself states no licence."},
    "a2_label_is_a_column": {"pass": true, "note": "Verified in the files: 'kind' is a literal column with values confirmation / denial / generic, balanced 115,113 / 115,113 in train. No construction needed for the yes/no task."},
    "a3_inputs_present": {"pass": true, "note": "Verified: coding DNA, transcript and amino-acid sequences are present as text, plus three precomputed embeddings per row (Evo2-7B 4,096 numbers, ESM-2 2,560, structure-aware PST 1,280), all finite, no lookups required."},
    "a4_units_stated": {"pass": true, "note": "Not a measurement dataset; the label is categorical and the embedding dimensions and source models are documented on the dataset card. Nothing ambiguous."},
    "a5_enough_rows": {"pass": true, "n_rows": 417835, "note": "Verified against the Hugging Face size endpoint and by reading the files: 345,927 train + 36,521 validation + 35,387 test = 417,835 rows over 17,732 genes."},
    "a6_fits_laptop": {"pass": true, "working_set": "~0.6 GB", "note": "8.1 GB on disk, but embeddings repeat across questions of the same gene; deduplicated to 17,732 unique genes it is about 0.6 GB. I read the text columns from the 7.2 GB file over range requests using ~2 GB of RAM."},
    "a7_counts_match_paper": {"pass": true, "note": "Files and dataset card agree exactly. Two small paper-vs-file mismatches: the paper says 'over 365,000' training pairs (files hold 345,927) and a test set of '34,743 questions' (files hold 35,387 total, 30,406 of them yes/no). Reconcilable, not confusing."},
    "a8_label_provenance": {"pass": false, "note": "FAILS, and I measured it. The questions were written by GPT-4.1, which knew the answer, and it encoded the answer in its writing style: 'could / might / may / potential' for yes, assertive specific phrasing for no. A bag-of-words model on question text alone, with zero biological input, reaches MCC 0.9449 on the authors' own test split versus the paper's headline 0.959. The label is therefore recoverable from a feature without any biology - leakage by construction. Separately, the free-text answers are themselves GPT-4.1 output, so training to generate them distils GPT-4.1 rather than learning biology."
  },
