  "backbone": {"name": "Evo2-7B (genomic language model), ESM-2 (protein language model) and PST (structure-aware protein transformer) embeddings are all precomputed in the files, so no backbone needs running", "freeze_and_head_viable": true, "hardware": "CPU laptop; embeddings already computed, a head on 0.6 GB of vectors trains in minutes", "licence": "Code MIT; paper CC-BY 4.0; the Hugging Face dataset repo states no licence, which is a gap if the data were reused"},
  "integration": {"partner_dataset": "UniProt-GOA human Gene Ontology annotations, and the Human Protein Atlas for subcellular location", "join_key": "HGNC gene symbol (the 'gene_name' column) via UniProt accession", "bigger_question_one_sentence": "Does a genomic language model's view of the coding DNA add anything to protein function prediction beyond what a protein language model already sees in the amino-acid sequence?"},
  "controls": {"positive_control": "Predict amino-acid sequence length or molecular weight from the ESM-2 embedding - must be near-perfect or the plumbing is broken.", "negative_control": "The one that killed this: strip all biological input and train on question wording alone. I ran it; it scores MCC 0.9449, so the benchmark fails its own negative control.", "external_validation": "UniProt-GOA annotations and CAFA benchmark splits exist for far more proteins than the 17,732 genes here, joinable on gene symbol."},
  "honest_claim_h3": "A model trained on this dataset would honestly only be claiming that it can tell apart two styles of machine-written question - hedging phrasing for true protein-function associations, assertive phrasing for false ones - because that alone gets 97% of the yes/no answers right without any DNA, protein or structure input.",
  "plain_idea": {
    "one_liner": "Use coding DNA, protein sequence and predicted 3D structure to answer yes-or-no questions about what a human protein does.",
    "train_on": "Each example is one natural-language question about an unnamed human protein, paired with three precomputed numeric fingerprints of that protein: its coding DNA (Evo2-7B), its amino-acid sequence (ESM-2) and its AlphaFold-predicted structure (PST). I verified 417,835 examples covering 17,732 human genes.",
    "predict": "Whether the function described in the question really is annotated for that protein (yes) or not (no). The true answer comes from Gene Ontology annotations for the gene, rewritten into plain English by GPT-4.1.",
    "why_worthwhile": "If it worked, it would show that reading a gene's DNA adds information about protein function beyond the protein sequence itself - which is the open question behind every genomic language model. As built, the benchmark cannot answer that, and demonstrating why is the useful result."
  },
  "crowded_field_case": {
    "more_or_better_data": {"answer": "partly", "why": "17,732 human genes with Evo2-7B DNA embeddings precomputed is genuinely rare and could not be reproduced on a laptop, but the function labels themselves are just Gene Ontology, which function-prediction models such as DeepGO-SE already train on for far more proteins across many species."},
    "confidence_score": {"answer": "no", "why": "There are no replicate measurements and no noise floor: each question has one machine-written answer, and nothing in the files supports estimating how uncertain a prediction should be."},
    "models_beatable": {"answer": "no", "why": "Beating the reported MCC 0.959 is meaningless, because I reached 0.9449 with bag-of-words on question wording alone. The number measures writing style, so improving it is not evidence about any model."},
    "guaranteed_improvement": {"answer": "no", "why": "The condition that fails is the evaluation split: the target itself is contaminated, so no added data can make predictions on this benchmark more meaningful. Running the released Genolator as a baseline would also need a GPU."}
