  "publication_target": null,
  "pitch": null,
  "ml_idea": "The dataset as published cannot support an honest model: the yes/no target is readable from question wording. The only defensible use is to throw away the GPT-4.1 question text and treat the files as a free feature store - 17,732 human genes with Evo2-7B DNA, ESM-2 sequence and PST structure embeddings already computed (about 0.6 GB deduplicated) - then attach Gene Ontology labels from UniProt-GOA and ask whether the DNA embedding adds anything over the protein embedding. That is a different project with an externally sourced label, and it should be evaluated on its own terms rather than on this benchmark.",
  "effort": {
    "hours_to_feature_table": 2,
    "working_set_gb": 0.6,
    "needs_gpu": false,
    "peak_ram_gb": 2,
    "embedding_hours": 0,
    "cv_run_minutes": 3,
    "compute_note": "Timed, not guessed: I downloaded 127 MB of samples, streamed two text columns from the 7.2 GB train file over HTTP range requests, and trained the text-only model on 230,226 rows in a single call of a few minutes using about 2 GB of RAM. Embedding hours are zero because all three backbones' outputs ship in the files.",
    "cheapest_killing_check": "Train any text classifier on the 'prompt' column alone, with every biological column deleted, and compare to the paper's MCC. I did exactly this: 0.9449 versus a headline 0.959. It takes under an hour and kills the project."
  },
  "verdict": "reject",
  "verdict_reason": "Fails Part A8 (label provenance), verified in the files rather than inferred. A bag-of-words model on the question wording alone - no DNA, no protein sequence, no structure, no gene identity - reaches MCC 0.9449 on the authors' own gene-disjoint test split, beating every baseline in the paper (XGBoost 0.913, GPT-4.1 0.179) and matching the second-best Genolator variant (0.948) against a headline of 0.959. GPT-4.1 wrote the true questions in hedging language and the false ones in assertive language, so the benchmark measures style, not biology; the free-text answers are also GPT-4.1 output, so generative training would distil that model. Everything else about the release is exemplary - open, documented to the column, counts matching, three expensive backbone embeddings precomputed for 17,732 human genes - which is why the only salvage is to discard the questions and relabel the embeddings from UniProt-GOA. Worth noting separately: this leakage result is a short, publishable comment in its own right (a Matters Arising or a Bioinformatics note), since the authors never ran a question-only ablation."
}
